#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175688 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "464bf015-9f06-5aaf-8fdd-fd419d351b02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "7fbb5683-c98c-5903-aad7-b2b729196def" :question/math-academy-id "e-10118" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/ef/ef996369f785405f40f576aafc3b0ec3a5045a38ee36636908c3ea91f976d94f.svg) \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $y\\gt 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0 \\leq x \\leq 2,\\;  y \\gt 1$ and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b43fd1a1622f406b7679d9f0f34552e046b9bc94b75a3504677f30b347d283.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq x, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(u+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xv]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2}+\\frac{x{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({x}^{2}+x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b96ef42-9581-56c7-9b09-b1ede3234b32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of the Joint CDF at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "77be8895-aa13-5dba-90ff-980840ec2f3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9221" :question/problem "![](images/46/46508c4856bb4a5086f7b21c3bf65758a0f9157179c661e63ae42944bd252cb9.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/9e/9eb73315957a9315c2ec10cbfa5e86c3f0bdd01617e8e68b3b1a8039b570b280.svg)\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/aa/aa4e587b8951f264d1f22392fd8fea7ae3397010376b82bbcd3ffab0d8dc50b8.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = P\\left(X<                2, Y<                \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{5/2} \\int_{-\\infty}^{2} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 2 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{u}v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{1/2}v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{[\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}v]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{[\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{3/2}v]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}\\frac{2}{5}v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{5}]}_{2}^{5/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175860 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1a96a21-f658-590f-a38b-deffd2f74145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Geometrically Using a CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "3d1ac1d5-1e37-57fc-8979-b727cb24fac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9222" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,5) = 0.9, \\quad\nF(5,2) = 0.3, \\quad\nF(0,5) = 0.45, \\quad\nF(0,2) = 0.15.\n$$\n\nFind $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 5, 2 \\leq Y \\leq 5).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}.$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 5,2\\le Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 2)}} \\\\  & =F(5,5)-F(5,2)-F(0,5)+F(0,2) \\\\  & =0.9-0.3-0.45+0.15 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175891 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca60661f-8374-58c5-830b-1202a2e008c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF: Simple Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "bed3e5a1-ab6e-51ee-8924-0dec972d0f33" :question/math-academy-id "e-9838" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/f7/f700fb9e7da84b3eb8d162847cada4ad69ae96b48da8b6e31f461272c19d0a17.svg)\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x > 1,\\; y > 4.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x > 1,\\; y >4,$ and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/0e/0e8ad9c26a96468741d20e886a3c18c56e248e529e90fa5908b0b472efd01e75.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta = D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $\\Delta$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b96ef42-9581-56c7-9b09-b1ede3234b32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of the Joint CDF at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da9eb2d0-96bb-559d-b134-c96c4f67c9f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173915} {:answer/id #uuid "e7450f20-683f-5b29-9b91-ba327d9aa516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173916} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbf191a-72d9-501b-82e3-2f6735f697ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173917} {:answer/id #uuid "2e457fee-1f1f-5f5d-a4f4-5fb241ee3b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173918} {:answer/id #uuid "77d5ff7b-faf5-58c7-a6e7-ad993dda120c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dbf191a-72d9-501b-82e3-2f6735f697ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173917} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6870a21-257d-5058-aa1a-18345227a06c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2996061a-7fb9-5ed0-bf07-62fdec460468" :question/math-academy-id "q-111909" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c64cfa701997c2b9e5e54f7eb77afb9927b878065af9c32fed86f09f140a4ec4.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5}).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/05/056fcbe25f83f488e1a88988d577535d0ef3776442f83e7d81560c6145f466a3.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5})=P(X<\\frac{1}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{5})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{5}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(u+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=0}^{u=1/2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{v}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{v}{8}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{40}+\\frac{1}{100}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1a96a21-f658-590f-a38b-deffd2f74145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Geometrically Using a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8761a460-67af-5891-a904-d0939782ea8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174401} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d4516d-450b-5883-8c5a-7161fd35a982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174402} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8270aa-7e49-540a-90df-23f01ae7bb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174403} {:answer/id #uuid "ef541986-52dc-5f53-9738-ce885957d53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174404} {:answer/id #uuid "749f8fc6-2978-5577-b15b-34a726bf4f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef541986-52dc-5f53-9738-ce885957d53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174404} :answer-field/id #uuid "752a1883-1da9-5746-a3d2-ac250438a8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ec3b2b4-bdd6-5bb0-988c-5cf4e342d853" :question/math-academy-id "q-137970" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(10,9)=0.7,\\,F(9,5)=0.4,\\,F(10,5)=0.2.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 10,5\\le Y\\le 9).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/a3/a3f2d39dd5b14494c726d5bc758fb9d89c5cdc18831fc900cae68da4cf5b6dcf.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/fa/fa3c9972c42d877a17609ead70776226ab4effccc991ecece596f542efea8cad.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10,5\\le Y\\le 9) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,5) \\\\  & =0.7-0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca60661f-8374-58c5-830b-1202a2e008c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF: Simple Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7af3a78-2189-55f7-924b-87ae9c355a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174497} {:answer/id #uuid "b05955a7-09e3-533e-be48-b11c1351ed2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174498} {:answer/id #uuid "3255e560-bc47-508d-8647-48857909d6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523357} {:answer/id #uuid "747225ec-e6bd-529a-b8f8-19e90acd91dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523492} {:answer/id #uuid "d12e9958-4ff9-5e58-90db-4cd906dea6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05955a7-09e3-533e-be48-b11c1351ed2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174498} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8629fc7-417a-57b7-99e4-432922e8f5bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91c3afe4-eca4-53cf-a37b-d909dbee3477" :question/math-academy-id "q-143568" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,\\,y>3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1,\\,y>3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/67/677dee4fb11cc609f089c8660ee16da5f9ac7572b27c9b0a3e97818e870b8db9.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $Δ$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca60661f-8374-58c5-830b-1202a2e008c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF: Simple Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccf84302-8540-5660-86b9-39475e7f3b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174504} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf027e2-292e-5dd7-9aed-18cd61b400fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174506} {:answer/id #uuid "906a169c-2edf-5f14-a67e-d3e7a3dc6d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523557} {:answer/id #uuid "a291d4a2-1c32-59ce-a889-698e12ba88cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523596} {:answer/id #uuid "f2954d4e-2ef2-52c9-a341-a49445314421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf84302-8540-5660-86b9-39475e7f3b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174504} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a226a41-ce63-5812-88a6-7616c15f80da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09afba65-133d-5717-8265-02f123f360fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143569" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,\\,1<y<3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0,\\,1<y<3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/30/3035c8fb56868a761b7c57d0f86077b796fe573ac289726414bc5b75ea91ab58.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b96ef42-9581-56c7-9b09-b1ede3234b32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of the Joint CDF at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5da53a-1db4-5289-abe3-b4086691e9a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523276} {:answer/id #uuid "39315a2d-6ace-5432-91f2-e18c46b76e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523373} {:answer/id #uuid "798df700-363e-5cc5-8534-6ccc98b7f5df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523503} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba9022d-dd18-5b7c-bc84-be4052ae1cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523510} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1c3cb0-0b20-5706-8e4d-e09a3517859d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39315a2d-6ace-5432-91f2-e18c46b76e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523373} :answer-field/id #uuid "be65f47b-87aa-5df5-8d18-766c3d2afc54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c8173cc-49c9-5f6d-b758-9380f0f1613b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143618" :question/problem "![](images/48/483c019e9c68f857379192d2b40ab84803c4360ca4e0158adc0a8a3bc1636c4e.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/ff/ffba85fc6ae71a1a5c250a90d686e8852c77756eebd228f3d3a2a9622cba01e1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<\\frac{1}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca60661f-8374-58c5-830b-1202a2e008c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF: Simple Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b19ecfd-a627-5697-a910-45b2a219ffd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523278} {:answer/id #uuid "52b58bdc-da06-5743-a21c-ed03d5de980f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523420} {:answer/id #uuid "690a125b-4b50-52a3-a7c4-98a2461af416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523469} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0ad379-42d9-5008-b164-1f7676c50f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523554} {:answer/id #uuid "f64a556c-0af0-5d31-a438-8927146cd5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b19ecfd-a627-5697-a910-45b2a219ffd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523278} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6198ea-775e-5856-8cd6-6fdd6ff63947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7fd97ca-9b98-50c0-b683-53f1d4a2aa1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143656" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,\\,y<0.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1,\\,y<0,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/12/1232f75c56b548896fc3c0371e36201eb24c71811d59d185440a964e393818be.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca60661f-8374-58c5-830b-1202a2e008c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF: Simple Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546f9ed9-007b-5e13-b2e5-6decca60ae2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523424} {:answer/id #uuid "63533c4d-d24b-59fa-ba96-39e3f6c1e18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523458} {:answer/id #uuid "69fa510b-bc1f-55f2-8bbc-4c5cbb321279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523472} {:answer/id #uuid "8679b652-02ec-5390-bd6e-d39302137cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523536} {:answer/id #uuid "f7dc7e2b-3b42-543d-837d-5072c514a774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "546f9ed9-007b-5e13-b2e5-6decca60ae2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523424} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3e14d43-5876-53c9-8df1-5009e3c0d56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ed10907-9632-53b1-9eec-d2c7cfd4e80a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143657" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,\\,y>\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0,\\,y>\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/4f/4f5cfe5e819f9479199ba1af6116627b31d03c88d8aa4c6e049bb375316c2c10.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca60661f-8374-58c5-830b-1202a2e008c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF: Simple Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c8f49-d804-5f98-8a95-def2b9471668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523273} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf84118-2059-5f36-ba3c-60ecf7e48225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523382} {:answer/id #uuid "94a42c42-afe5-541a-ba5c-8988133fd11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523567} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a87f55-8422-523f-8f58-b46dfbaf6a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523641} {:answer/id #uuid "dec1957e-5a0e-5556-92fc-2e137c12ed35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf84118-2059-5f36-ba3c-60ecf7e48225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523382} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1873516-1ea7-5025-ba34-62e81accf8f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f06b7511-18eb-5f50-9aea-3636371eb6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143658" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}, & -1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/19/19510c5eb927edbc93ff077f13e645820548e9074da0e1101d9ef641650642bb.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $Δ$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "464bf015-9f06-5aaf-8fdd-fd419d351b02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1095f72c-8905-575a-b1e8-c4f151588556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{4}+x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523290} {:answer/id #uuid "14c8cd64-1d0a-54bd-9709-4c36bc06ddae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{xy}{4}+x{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523298} {:answer/id #uuid "2494f35b-fb23-5fe3-a9a9-ec37d5670ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{4}+x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523327} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0afbaa-d117-5fda-b304-a0c098057560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523377} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e4bb67-f0ed-58a6-b574-7f6f135ea50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b0afbaa-d117-5fda-b304-a0c098057560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523377} :answer-field/id #uuid "10efcb21-a5fa-5adc-bb8a-dc7d1ccdc194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1b6ab32-2ee9-5286-82fb-e9633b19fc28" :question/math-academy-id "q-143693" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}({y}^{2}+xy), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfa6842026fc1635efe939525d60a9c83c89735a1a1e69d32d1b31c8431f02c.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{[\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}u+\\frac{{u}^{2}v}{2})]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{3}{5}(x{v}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{[\\frac{x{v}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "464bf015-9f06-5aaf-8fdd-fd419d351b02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209bfb2e-af2f-5d6a-840c-05fd1de20ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523317} {:answer/id #uuid "29c0dbe5-4efd-5b3d-8117-33fcf80cf8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523340} {:answer/id #uuid "415fdd0e-bb48-5e8c-82e5-fb1aeaf72830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523389} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdc4d77-589b-57d8-8d92-72fa69c4b672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523751} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1ed0e2-8bf5-5e15-a29a-2e70d2fbbef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c0dbe5-4efd-5b3d-8117-33fcf80cf8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523340} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e7581f6-409c-526a-8e01-7cfa799ca69b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e58401f9-a4c1-5465-9216-a1679c479a59" :question/math-academy-id "q-143695" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}x{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/e4/e4aef49bebaf250fa93e0ad1daccbad324bf714c7e5627f5245ea8387bc8da87.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{12}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{12}{v}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}u\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{12}{v}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1a96a21-f658-590f-a38b-deffd2f74145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Geometrically Using a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "537999eb-f702-5e5a-b808-1d5e4897e7d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523422} {:answer/id #uuid "880d5f55-fa39-5e77-a30f-7b1bc2813874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523539} {:answer/id #uuid "8d670cde-225e-5b9b-8c8d-2fc3c8a36857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523547} {:answer/id #uuid "c25461e4-3b0a-58dd-8b88-4a3086a23bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523660} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d43c59-6f14-5ddc-adb1-7ca44d5b7cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5d43c59-6f14-5ddc-adb1-7ca44d5b7cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523736} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aeeeffd-771c-5e22-8635-b25572ba714b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e662cd14-0105-5064-96eb-7a2f86c8b78d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143701" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(8,10)=\\frac{9}{10},\\,F(8,8)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,F(8,4)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/d3/d31cdb5e3f201d9dd5b3b622d883f67bb2c8bc0a5036905695a5cc5ceb3bda07.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,8)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/62/626d5c4cba7b41d6b6f2da77ca1d62c5bcd442990ce31cf630e69882dd6580b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,8)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 8)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(8,8)-F(8,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1a96a21-f658-590f-a38b-deffd2f74145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Geometrically Using a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1220b7d0-ad4f-50da-a224-1d43a9374c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523294} {:answer/id #uuid "297d7533-8470-571e-ba87-7ac9cbd8c6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523339} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4eb767-1066-5a82-b1b3-22a8a24eafab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523474} {:answer/id #uuid "84f8b6ae-7ca4-5a99-bf55-083348f055a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523535} {:answer/id #uuid "a89e5a41-027a-5495-9e5b-b017f651256b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f8b6ae-7ca4-5a99-bf55-083348f055a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523535} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e041ab-9982-589c-bc56-123e99e4c001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "419b7449-2db5-5f6c-9da4-4ea57433c3f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143703" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,5)=0.63,\\,F(0,5)=0.5,\\,F(-2,5)=0.27.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/03/0378964a9f167554e4faeba228ebae485de94dda83fbd6704a864a06b8abe77a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,5)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/9079baa6464df773cacb820a6bb4a8e2b8f4ecc19755619a9b5c191a0c48507f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 5)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(1,5)-F(-2,5) \\\\  & =0.63-0.27 \\\\  & =0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1a96a21-f658-590f-a38b-deffd2f74145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Geometrically Using a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8baf0c-cd7e-581a-93c7-c3900b854b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523314} {:answer/id #uuid "61c6ad87-fa8a-5d9c-8da2-1164e2fede9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523451} {:answer/id #uuid "62c8319f-eff2-571e-bcb6-00ff9af3442a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523456} {:answer/id #uuid "ed965288-4b09-507b-9790-a2fd183c8104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523754} {:answer/id #uuid "fe36d242-dab0-5d15-a25f-23aecf853bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8baf0c-cd7e-581a-93c7-c3900b854b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523314} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf65c66-4c8d-5602-8e69-2970d52a51bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ebdf296-e322-505e-9c4f-5149cacb6a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143707" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,4)=1,\\,F(5,1)=\\frac{2}{9},\\,F(4,4)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,F(4,1)=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/13/137e3d2e3344f5d1f57f36d470564a518c359bb46d5278382b7242e73999e80e.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,4).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(4,4).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,4)-F(5,1)-F(4,4)+F(4,1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "464bf015-9f06-5aaf-8fdd-fd419d351b02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b612e0-50c4-52a8-8946-ce06bbf20ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523304} {:answer/id #uuid "2c924db7-4b2c-5c7b-b0e5-9e7980807592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523345} {:answer/id #uuid "56b42f84-5509-5bcb-9980-f9dbb75f1963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523432} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0b9f91-9543-5641-bb77-95c2a131753a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({y}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523645} {:answer/id #uuid "da9bdc95-8e4f-54c4-8a48-14f10b9adc06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16b612e0-50c4-52a8-8946-ce06bbf20ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523304} :answer-field/id #uuid "845d7c96-a14e-530a-9ba3-0bbcb5ec1e7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61f58f19-0648-54c0-b55e-2caca7ea1507" :question/math-academy-id "q-143712" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>3$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/1f/1fbbe9e4728cd5aefcc456a6c8666e3e2e6c0d0bc832f471677d908cf6c012e3.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}v{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}v(1-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}\\frac{2}{3}v\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=1}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "464bf015-9f06-5aaf-8fdd-fd419d351b02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271ba238-8f69-525e-bb35-98eee22b398a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523332} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb1b36a-bb03-5898-8c00-7b52f0a1cf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523354} {:answer/id #uuid "4c52e4cf-f222-5624-9b6b-2a72692caecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+x-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523410} {:answer/id #uuid "65557329-98b5-5b38-93db-0154d8a2d280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523463} {:answer/id #uuid "bec80840-8b96-572a-96e8-64bd7c631522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "271ba238-8f69-525e-bb35-98eee22b398a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523332} :answer-field/id #uuid "93696133-c36c-56da-ad2a-2f1497fe6ebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e52f8c43-5915-56b1-841a-9a33ac8f7470" :question/math-academy-id "q-143716" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2}+y, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>1$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/12/12e26559137d7c494c5ddc6f1add55f646dd84236f355221042448ddeacd2879.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{x}u{v}^{2}+v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=1}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}+xv-\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}-v]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{v}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)}{2}+v(x-1)]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{3}({x}^{2}-1)}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{2}(x-1)}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{6}+\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "464bf015-9f06-5aaf-8fdd-fd419d351b02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Joint CDF from a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aaefb20-e0f8-53e5-a3f4-1888bb386733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523307} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcc536f-5146-5eb2-9bfc-3649d9de4a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(2{x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523511} {:answer/id #uuid "831f8dad-5a3f-5eed-a9cb-51007d1c3429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523532} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb81a74-72a1-5f19-bc38-b39dca6a150f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523715} {:answer/id #uuid "f71f5628-605d-5c10-95cc-aabea9c6e8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb81a74-72a1-5f19-bc38-b39dca6a150f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523715} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7929f6-25fe-54ba-932a-cb75244104ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27951774-1d49-5cba-9914-8523d1f1e8cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143717" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y>2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y>2$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/f5/f5ceaa24d74de28cc24de05ab352fbe4586c4c83630d6163f4871ccbe74e2cd2.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(1+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[u+\\frac{{u}^{2}v}{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[xv+\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+{x}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b96ef42-9581-56c7-9b09-b1ede3234b32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of the Joint CDF at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ba8c61-c212-52ee-888a-00441515fd5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523259} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea56258-291c-5f2c-9c35-b323b146d447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523520} {:answer/id #uuid "a61dcb51-4a14-5f5f-82c4-141f3d9ad22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523603} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa696f6-6f20-540d-a235-7ceafd2611d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523691} {:answer/id #uuid "e718edfc-229b-5e95-b802-2ff85ca21e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e718edfc-229b-5e95-b802-2ff85ca21e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523740} :answer-field/id #uuid "71eb090d-6e48-50da-bb66-1c6f0856fb96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bec72aa-1d05-5e53-b2ca-0df4a3dfbe51" :question/math-academy-id "q-143739" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a5/a5f876c9351040845fcc5e6839f4ca776fe22988a5e4c814d4a7b063692f083c.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(0,\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<0,Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=0}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(0+\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{3}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b96ef42-9581-56c7-9b09-b1ede3234b32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of the Joint CDF at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23310bfc-17e6-5580-a55e-e246c24629fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523323} {:answer/id #uuid "39d570d1-ff8c-5514-a7e3-6aca29e5688a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523375} {:answer/id #uuid "caccaafe-7667-5ccc-8d3c-ece95431f0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523677} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b551eb-8620-5f76-bb82-a5c648a8788e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523730} {:answer/id #uuid "f803b7aa-c839-5645-a503-8f8949428530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2b551eb-8620-5f76-bb82-a5c648a8788e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523730} :answer-field/id #uuid "8afeb2a5-b592-5221-9a34-afffd9137833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d405caca-881b-5a05-9419-094e813c3d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143740" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/dd/dd7024bcd41ce8312f484763e0988ebcceb54e7154cd8ee3c209991bce5727c1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<-\\frac{3}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b96ef42-9581-56c7-9b09-b1ede3234b32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of the Joint CDF at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f1848b-2067-5ef4-a435-25645f66885f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523258} {:answer/id #uuid "48826592-f39f-5544-b033-af627b3ec9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523403} {:answer/id #uuid "9a77a2f3-aba1-542a-a9a0-676775a31c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523576} {:answer/id #uuid "b254c28d-2f4a-5f0f-92fd-f787e8b75821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523631} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9a463b-d985-5b34-bc90-9079f8752c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a77a2f3-aba1-542a-a9a0-676775a31c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523576} :answer-field/id #uuid "0348f5f0-90e0-585b-8e6d-413d4121229b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c246e56e-54f4-5ef4-b7e3-0ed423a3b4da" :question/math-academy-id "q-143742" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/53/53d0466b98c4b9b2704cc2f7610e7f28cee6ec27aaf2ea94be75c5b3f32a8257.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(1,\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<1,Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}[\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2}-(-\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2})]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}3{v}^{2}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
